### Name and Version

```
% build/bin/llama-cli --version
version: 10073 (91d2fc387)
built with AppleClang 21.0.0.21000101 for Darwin arm64
```

### Operating systems

Mac

### Which llama.cpp modules do you know to be affected?

libllama (core library)

### Command line

```shell

```

### Problem description & steps to reproduce

On macOS with Metal, if no prompt was ever run, calling `llama_model_free` does not unwire the wired memory. Memory pressure on my Mac stays high.

<img width="590" height="102" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/35f3fc79-deea-42c5-8c90-777d98c09338" />

Running any prompt before `llama_model_free` solves the problem. Wired memory goes down almost immediately after `llama_model_free`.

<img width="594" height="98" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e2474875-ddd2-4699-8bb9-9b952eeaa262" />

**Reproduction:**

```c
#include <unistd.h>
#include <llama.h>

int main() {
    llama_backend_init();

    struct llama_model* model = llama_model_load_from_file("gemma-4-26B_q4_0-it.gguf", llama_model_default_params());
    llama_model_free(model);

    llama_backend_free();

    sleep(20);

    return 0;
}
```

Then run `top -l 1 | grep PhysMem` during the 20 seconds of sleep and after the program exits.

During the `sleep(20)`, the output is:

```
PhysMem: 44G used (16G wired, 888M compressor), 2843M unused.
```

After the program exits, the output is:

```
PhysMem: 44G used (3000M wired, 888M compressor), 2912M unused.
```

13GB of memory seem to only be unwired after the program exits.

**Debugging:**

When running with `GGML_METAL_NO_RESIDENCY=1`, this problem is not visible. However, that's not good for performance.

I added some logging and found that [`endResidency`](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/91d2fc387529940230555abd297a8b5e99737d3f/ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-device.m#L1484) was called properly on the residency set in my tests. `device.currentAllocatedSize` went down afterward. Wired memory, however, did not go down.

With the help of Fable 5, I found that doing **any GPU work** in the process before `llama_model_free` causes the system to unwire the memory (even GPU work that is unrelated to the residency set).

<details><summary>Fable-generated patch that does dummy GPU work and causes the memory to be unwired</summary>
<p>

```diff
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-device.m b/ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-device.m
index 80e47f2c2..fbe52831b 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-device.m
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-device.m
@@ -869,6 +869,31 @@ ggml_metal_device_t ggml_metal_device_init(int device) {
                 dev->rsets = nil;
             }

+            // submit a dummy command buffer with non-empty work so that the OS starts tracking the
+            // process for wired memory accounting. without this, memory wired via residency sets
+            // whose buffers are never referenced by any GPU work stays wired after the buffers are
+            // freed, until the process exits
+            if (dev->props.use_residency_sets && dev->mtl_queue != nil) {
+                @autoreleasepool {
+                    id<MTLBuffer> buf = [dev->mtl_device newBufferWithLength:16 options:MTLResourceStorageModeShared];
+
+                    id<MTLCommandBuffer> cmd_buf = [dev->mtl_queue commandBufferWithUnretainedReferences];
+
+                    {
+                        id<MTLBlitCommandEncoder> encoder = [cmd_buf blitCommandEncoder];
+
+                        [encoder fillBuffer:buf range:NSMakeRange(0, 16) value:0];
+
+                        [encoder endEncoding];
+                    }
+
+                    [cmd_buf commit];
+                    [cmd_buf waitUntilCompleted];
+
+                    [buf release];
+                }
+            }
+
             // print MTL GPU family:
             GGML_LOG_INFO("%s: GPU name:   %s (%s)\n", __func__, dev->props.name, dev->props.desc);

```

</p>
</details>

(This patch is the only AI-generated part of this issue. The rest was hand-written by me.)

If maintainers have an idea for how to solve this properly, I'm happy to work on the implementation of a fix (hand-written, of course).

